steps.body:
A backtest loads a day of ticks, builds features from them, and feeds both to a simulator. At 10^7 ticks of 40 bytes, that buffer is 400 MB. A copy at a stage boundary allocates another 400 MB and writes every byte: copying 10^6 ticks took a few milliseconds here, so 10^7 costs tens of milliseconds, at every stage of every run. A `std::vector` move copies three pointers, about a microsecond or less here at any size. Both times are rough, since the sandbox runs x86-64 emulated.

So each stage's signature says what it does with the buffer:

| Stage | Signature | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| loader | `std::vector<Tick> load(int n)` | makes the buffer and returns it by value |
| features | `Features build_features(std::vector<Tick> ticks)` | takes it over: a sink |
| simulator | `double simulate(const Features& f)` | only reads it |

Inside `build_features`, the last step is `f.ticks = std::move(ticks);`, so the buffer goes on into the result, and `return f;` is NRVO.

How do you prove nothing was copied? A vector's elements live in one heap buffer, and `data()` returns its address. A move hands that buffer over, so the new owner's `data()` equals the old one, and the moved-from vector is left empty, which a vector's move constructor guarantees. A copy gets a new buffer at a new address. Comparing the two pointers, without ever printing them, is exact evidence:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

struct Tick {
    int64_t ts_ns;          // nanoseconds since midnight
    double bid, ask;        // prices
    double bid_sz, ask_sz;  // sizes
};

struct Features {
    std::vector<Tick> ticks;
    std::vector<double> mids;
};

std::vector<Tick> load(int n) {
    return std::vector<Tick>(n, Tick{34200000000000, 99.5, 100.5, 300, 200});
}

Features build_features(std::vector<Tick> ticks) {
    Features f;
    for (const Tick& t : ticks) {
        f.mids.push_back((t.bid + t.ask) / 2);
    }
    f.ticks = std::move(ticks);
    return f;
}

int main() {
    {{code}}
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
std::vector<Tick> raw = load(1000);
const Tick* buffer = raw.data();
Features f = build_features(std::move(raw));
std::cout << std::boolalpha << (f.ticks.data() == buffer) << ' ' << raw.size() << '\n';  // true 0
```

Forget the `std::move` and everything still compiles and runs: `build_features(raw)` quietly copies the whole buffer, and `raw` keeps its ticks.

