A wrapper in front of the order book, one that audits, timestamps, or checks risk before it calls `add`, should pass each order on at the cost its caller chose: a copy for an lvalue, a move for an rvalue. A `const T&` and a `T&&` overload of every wrapper doubles the code, and doubles it again for each parameter. A template writes it once:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

struct Tracker {
    inline static int copies = 0;
    inline static int moves = 0;

    Tracker() = default;
    Tracker(const Tracker&) {
        ++copies;
        std::cout << "copy\n";
    }
    Tracker(Tracker&&) noexcept {
        ++moves;
        std::cout << "move\n";
    }
    Tracker& operator=(const Tracker&) {
        ++copies;
        std::cout << "copy\n";
        return *this;
    }
    Tracker& operator=(Tracker&&) noexcept {
        ++moves;
        std::cout << "move\n";
        return *this;
    }
};

using Order = Tracker;

struct Book {
    std::vector<Order> orders;
    Book() {
        orders.reserve(4);
    }
    void add(const Order& o) {
        orders.push_back(o);
    }
    void add(Order&& o) {
        orders.push_back(std::move(o));
    }
};

void audit(const Order&) {
    std::cout << "audited\n";
}

{{code}}

int main() {
    Book book;
    Order resting;
    audit_then_add(book, resting);
    audit_then_add(book, Order{});
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
template <typename T>
void audit_then_add(Book& book, T&& order) {
    audit(order);
    book.add(std::forward<T>(order));
}
```

When `T` is deduced, `T&&` is a **forwarding reference**, and it binds to anything:

- Called with an lvalue `Order`, `T` is deduced as `Order&`, and `Order& &&` collapses to `Order&`.
- Called with an rvalue, `T` is plain `Order`, and the parameter is `Order&&`.

The collapsing rule is that any `&` wins: only `&&` with `&&` stays `&&`.

Inside the function, `order` has a name, so it is an lvalue either way, and passing plain `order` on always copies. `std::forward<T>(order)` restores the category: it casts to an rvalue only when `T` isn't a reference, which is exactly when the caller passed an rvalue. Use `std::forward` on forwarding references, and `std::move` on plain `T&&` parameters and on objects you own. `std::is_lvalue_reference_v<T>` from `<type_traits>` shows which way `T` went.

Not every `&&` in a template is a forwarding reference. `void add(Order&& o)` deduces nothing, and in `template <typename T> void f(std::vector<T>&& v)` the parameter isn't plain `T&&`, so both take only rvalues. `auto&&` is one, though: `for (auto&& x : range)` binds each element whatever it is.

