steps.body:
A book that keeps orders in a `std::vector<Order>` can build each one right inside the vector. `push_back` takes a finished object, while `emplace_back` takes the constructor's arguments. With `Order` holding a `Tracker`, and capacity reserved so growth doesn't count:

| Call | What happens | Count |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `push_back(Order(7, 101.25, 300))` | builds a temporary, moves it in | 1 move |
| `push_back({7, 101.25, 300})` | the same, from braces | 1 move |
| `emplace_back(7, 101.25, 300)` | builds the order in place | nothing |
| `push_back(o)` or `emplace_back(o)` | the same thing: a copy | 1 copy |
| `emplace_back(std::move(o))` | the same as `push_back`: a move | 1 move |

So `emplace_back` only wins when you hand it constructor arguments. Given an existing object, the two are identical.

`emplace_back` is built on a forwarding pack. `typename... Args` declares a **parameter pack**, any number of types, and `Args&&... args` makes each argument a forwarding reference. `std::forward<Args>(args)...` expands to one forwarded argument per element of the pack, separated by commas, and *Variadic Templates and Fold Expressions*, two lessons on, does much more with packs. Your own factory looks the same:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

struct Order {
    Order(int i, double p, int q) : id(i), price(p), qty(q) {}
    int id;
    double price;
    int qty;
};

{{code}}

int main() {
    std::vector<Order> orders;
    Order& o = emplace_order(orders, 7, 101.25, 300);
    std::cout << o.id << ' ' << o.price << ' ' << o.qty << ' ' << orders.size() << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
template <typename... Args>
Order& emplace_order(std::vector<Order>& orders, Args&&... args) {
    orders.emplace_back(std::forward<Args>(args)...);  // one std::forward per argument
    return orders.back();
}
```

`std::make_unique<T>(args...)` works exactly this way. Three C++17 details:

- `emplace_back` calls a constructor with parentheses, so an aggregate with no constructor, such as `Tick`, can't use it with arguments. Write `ticks.push_back({...})`. C++20 lifts that rule.
- It calls `explicit` constructors too: on a `std::vector<std::vector<Tick>>`, `days.emplace_back(1000000)` quietly builds a day of 10^6 zeroed ticks, where `push_back(1000000)` would not compile.
- It returns a reference to the new element.

A `Tick` owns no heap memory, so moving one copies its 40 bytes, exactly like copying it. Moves only save work for types that own a buffer.

